 "article": {
  "title": "Tea: A Leaf That Travelled the World",
  "topic": "人文历史",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 330,
  "paras": [
   "For more than a thousand years, tea has been much more than a drink in China. It began as a medicine in the mountains of the southwest, and by the Tang Dynasty it had become a daily habit from the palace to the village. People drank it at home, offered it to guests, and used it in important ceremonies. A single leaf, it seemed, could hold the whole culture of a nation.",
   "Yet the story of tea does not stop at the border. During the Tang and Song dynasties, merchants carried tea along the Silk Road, together with silk, porcelain and ideas. It travelled by camel across the desert, by boat down the great rivers, and finally by sea to Japan, Korea, Persia and, much later, to Europe. In the seventeenth century, tea arrived in London, where it quickly became a fashion among rich families before it reached ordinary ones.",
   "What made this journey possible was not only trade but curiosity. Foreign visitors were curious about the strange bitter drink and the rituals that surrounded it; Chinese merchants, in turn, were eager to learn about new markets and new tastes. Wherever tea went, it changed. The Japanese developed the tea ceremony, the British added milk and sugar, and the Mongols mixed it with butter and salt. The same leaf produced very different cultures, which suggests that exchange never simply copies — it transforms.",
   "Today tea is the second most popular drink in the world after water, and the old routes that once carried it have become a symbol of cultural exchange. The lesson of this small leaf is simple and still useful: a nation that is willing to share what it has, and curious enough to learn from others, will always find friends along the road."
  ],
  "zh": "茶在中国早已不只是一杯饮料：它从西南山区的药材，变成唐宋时期从宫廷到村落的日常，又沿着丝绸之路随商队走遍世界。让这段旅程成为可能的，不只是贸易，更是好奇心——同一片叶子在不同的土地上被改造成日本茶道、英式奶茶、蒙古酥油茶。今天茶是世界第二大饮品，这条古老的商路也成了文化交流的象征：愿意分享、乐于学习的人和国家，总能在路上遇到朋友。",
  "hard": [
   {
    "en": "What made this journey possible was not only trade but curiosity.",
    "cn": "让这段旅程成为可能的，不仅是贸易，更是好奇心。",
    "why": "主语从句 What... 作主语 + not only... but (also)... 并列；写作可直接套用「What made X possible was not only A but B」。"
